WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:04.210
Part C, find the
average value of f

00:04.210 --> 00:07.100
on the interval negative 1 to 1.

00:07.100 --> 00:09.020
So the average
value of a function

00:09.020 --> 00:11.410
over an interval is just
going to be-- so let's

00:11.410 --> 00:13.030
just write average.

00:13.030 --> 00:14.800
The average value
of our function

00:14.800 --> 00:19.525
is just going to be the integral
over the interval negative 1

00:19.525 --> 00:27.200
to 1 of f of x, d of x,
divided by our change in x.

00:27.200 --> 00:28.740
Sorry, this is from
negative 1 to 1.

00:35.780 --> 00:40.120
And our change in x
is 1 minus negative 1.

00:40.120 --> 00:44.810
So this is going to be equal
to 1/2 times the integral.

00:44.810 --> 00:47.579
And here, f of x is
piecewise-defined.

00:47.579 --> 00:49.370
So what we can do is
break up this integral

00:49.370 --> 00:50.430
into two intervals.

00:50.430 --> 00:53.655
We can say the integral
of f of x from negative 1

00:53.655 --> 00:58.900
to 0 of f of x dx plus-- let
me just write it this way

00:58.900 --> 01:02.540
so we don't have to
keep rewriting the 1/2.

01:02.540 --> 01:05.810
I'll use brackets--
plus the integral

01:05.810 --> 01:11.530
from 0 to 1 of f of x dx.

01:11.530 --> 01:13.380
And the reason why I
broke it up like this

01:13.380 --> 01:16.770
is because this function
has a different definition,

01:16.770 --> 01:20.150
or it's piecewise-defined.

01:20.150 --> 01:23.460
It's different when we're
less than or equal to 0,

01:23.460 --> 01:25.730
versus when we are
greater than 0.

01:25.730 --> 01:28.090
So that's why I like to
break it up this way.

01:28.090 --> 01:31.990
So then we get, this
is equal to 1/2 times--

01:31.990 --> 01:35.260
and in big brackets like this.

01:35.260 --> 01:37.180
This first part
right over here, we

01:37.180 --> 01:40.980
can write as the integral
from negative 1 to 0.

01:40.980 --> 01:43.300
What is f of x between
negative 1 and 0?

01:43.300 --> 01:51.010
It's 1 minus 2 sine of x dx.

01:51.010 --> 01:54.420
And then plus this
thing right over here.

01:54.420 --> 01:58.550
Plus the integral from 0 to 1.

01:58.550 --> 02:02.500
And what is our function
when it's between 0 and 1?

02:02.500 --> 02:08.259
It's e to the negative 4x dx.

02:08.259 --> 02:11.660
And now we can do each of
these integrals separately.

02:11.660 --> 02:16.080
And so this is going
to be equal to 1/2--

02:16.080 --> 02:19.270
and once again, I'll do a big
open bracket right over here.

02:19.270 --> 02:22.070
My pen is getting loose, let me
tie on the front a little bit

02:22.070 --> 02:22.570
better.

02:22.570 --> 02:23.570
There you go.

02:23.570 --> 02:25.140
All right, back to work.

02:25.140 --> 02:26.830
So 1/2, open brackets.

02:26.830 --> 02:28.690
And now, let's take
the antiderivative

02:28.690 --> 02:31.640
of 1 minus 2 sine
of x, antiderivative

02:31.640 --> 02:34.870
of 1 with respect
to x is just x.

02:34.870 --> 02:36.250
Negative 2 sine of x.

02:36.250 --> 02:40.770
Well, the derivative of cosine
of x is negative sine of x.

02:40.770 --> 02:44.210
So this is just going
to be 2 cosine of x.

02:44.210 --> 02:45.670
And you can verify that.

02:45.670 --> 02:47.670
Derivative of cosine of
x is negative sine of x.

02:47.670 --> 02:50.640
You have the 2 out front, so
it's negative 2 sine of x.

02:50.640 --> 02:55.890
So we're going to evaluate
that at 0 and at negative 1.

02:55.890 --> 02:58.980
And to that, we
are going to add--

02:58.980 --> 03:01.350
so let's do this definite
integral over here.

03:01.350 --> 03:05.480
The antiderivative of
e to the negative 4x

03:05.480 --> 03:13.220
is going to be equal to negative
e to the negative 4x over 4.

03:13.220 --> 03:17.390
And the way you realize that is,
if this was just an e to the x,

03:17.390 --> 03:20.070
then it's antiderivative
would just be e to the x.

03:20.070 --> 03:22.640
If you have an e
to the negative 4x,

03:22.640 --> 03:25.130
then you know that whatever
its antiderivative is

03:25.130 --> 03:29.740
is going to be, essentially,
an e to the negative 4x.

03:29.740 --> 03:31.510
But when you take
its derivative,

03:31.510 --> 03:33.301
you're going to have
to take the derivative

03:33.301 --> 03:35.600
of the negative 4x part
because of the chain rule.

03:35.600 --> 03:38.000
And so you're going to have
a negative 4 that comes out.

03:38.000 --> 03:41.480
But we don't see a
negative 4 over here,

03:41.480 --> 03:44.520
so we're obviously going to
have to divide by negative 4

03:44.520 --> 03:45.710
so it cancels out.

03:45.710 --> 03:47.900
Another way to
think about it is,

03:47.900 --> 03:50.930
we could have rewritten
we this thing.

03:50.930 --> 03:55.720
So this is equal to e
to the negative 4x dx.

03:55.720 --> 03:58.280
This is exactly
what our problem is,

03:58.280 --> 04:00.299
what we need to take
the definite integral.

04:00.299 --> 04:01.840
So then, it becomes
completely clear.

04:01.840 --> 04:04.340
So that the derivative of this
thing is sitting around here,

04:04.340 --> 04:06.810
we could put a
negative 4 over here.

04:06.810 --> 04:09.920
But you can't just willy-nilly
throw a negative 4 there.

04:09.920 --> 04:12.850
You would have to put a
negative 1/4 outside of it

04:12.850 --> 04:17.029
in order for it to be
negative 1/4 times negative 4.

04:17.029 --> 04:20.250
You're just multiplying by 1,
which doesn't change the value.

04:20.250 --> 04:26.370
And then here, you'd clearly
see that this right over here

04:26.370 --> 04:30.470
is the derivative of
e to the negative 4x.

04:30.470 --> 04:32.930
So that you'd have e
to the negative 4x.

04:32.930 --> 04:35.110
The antiderivative of this
is e to the negative 4x.

04:35.110 --> 04:37.560
And then you have this
negative 1/4 over here.

04:37.560 --> 04:40.867
So either way, hopefully
that makes sense.

04:40.867 --> 04:42.450
We go into more
detail of that earlier

04:42.450 --> 04:43.999
in the calculus playlist.

04:43.999 --> 04:46.040
So then this is from--
we're going to evaluate it

04:46.040 --> 04:54.830
at 1 and at 0, and then we
want to close the brackets.

04:54.830 --> 04:56.340
And so, what do we have here?

04:56.340 --> 04:57.540
This is equal to 1/2.

04:57.540 --> 05:00.090
Once again, open the brackets.

05:00.090 --> 05:02.140
If you evaluate
all of this at 0,

05:02.140 --> 05:04.830
you get 0 plus 2 cosine of 0.

05:04.830 --> 05:06.630
Cosine of 0 is 1.

05:06.630 --> 05:09.580
So you just get a 2 when
you evaluate it at the 0.

05:09.580 --> 05:11.030
So you get a 2.

05:11.030 --> 05:13.450
And then from that, we
want to subtract whatever

05:13.450 --> 05:16.530
we get when we evaluate
it at negative 1.

05:16.530 --> 05:21.790
We want to subtract negative
1 plus 2 cosine of negative 1.

05:26.320 --> 05:28.310
So this whole thing
right over here

05:28.310 --> 05:31.240
evaluates to this whole
thing right over there.

05:31.240 --> 05:33.670
And then we have plus.

05:33.670 --> 05:36.400
We want to evaluate this at 1.

05:36.400 --> 05:41.530
So this gives us negative
e to the negative 4.

05:41.530 --> 05:46.620
Because negative 4x, when
x is 1, is negative 4.

05:46.620 --> 05:47.730
Over 4.

05:47.730 --> 05:49.670
And from that, we
need to subtract

05:49.670 --> 05:51.090
this thing evaluated at 0.

05:54.160 --> 05:59.430
So that's going to be
negative e to the 0 over 4.

05:59.430 --> 06:00.850
e to the 0 is 1.

06:00.850 --> 06:04.790
So that's just
negative 1 over 4.

06:04.790 --> 06:09.390
And once again, all of this is
going to be multiplied by 1/2.

06:09.390 --> 06:11.710
And now we just
have to simplify it.

06:11.710 --> 06:14.500
So this is equal to-- well, I'll
just still throw out the 1/2

06:14.500 --> 06:15.000
here.

06:15.000 --> 06:19.320
This is equal to 1/2 times--
so I'll do this in a new color

06:19.320 --> 06:19.820
here.

06:19.820 --> 06:22.070
Let me do it-- well I don't
have too many new colors.

06:22.070 --> 06:22.830
OK.

06:22.830 --> 06:25.440
So this is equal to 2 plus 1.

06:25.440 --> 06:28.820
So this and this together
is going to be equal to 3.

06:28.820 --> 06:31.390
And then you have
this negative outside.

06:31.390 --> 06:33.310
So that's why it was a plus 1.

06:33.310 --> 06:35.930
And then you have a
negative times a positive 2.

06:35.930 --> 06:39.750
So minus 2 cosine of negative 1.

06:39.750 --> 06:42.290
And then you have
plus-- or maybe I

06:42.290 --> 06:47.210
should say minus-- minus e
to the negative 4 over 4.

06:47.210 --> 06:48.880
And then you have plus 1/4.

06:52.140 --> 06:54.340
And then we want to
close our brackets.

06:54.340 --> 06:57.390
And then we could do one last
step of simplification here.

06:57.390 --> 07:00.270
3, we could add
the 3 to the 1/4.

07:00.270 --> 07:02.886
3 is the same
thing as 12 over 4.

07:02.886 --> 07:06.870
12 over 4 plus 1/4 is 13/4.

07:06.870 --> 07:14.190
So you have 13/4 minus 2
cosine of negative 1 minus

07:14.190 --> 07:16.680
e to the negative 4 over 4.

07:16.680 --> 07:19.970
And then, of course, you have
your 1/2 sitting out here.

07:19.970 --> 07:23.100
And it's not the most
beautiful or simple thing.

07:23.100 --> 07:24.210
But this is our answer.

07:24.210 --> 07:28.430
This is the average value of
f of x over that interval.
